<Layout title="Modelling rules and upper-ontology lenses" description="SPDTF modelling rules for identity, classes, controlled values, relationships, reuse, mappings and candidate upper-ontology methods.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF" variant="brand" updated="Candidate modelling rules · upper ontology not selected" />
  <h1>Modelling rules and upper-ontology lenses</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The model starts with identity and business distinctions, then chooses the smallest
    construct that preserves them. External frameworks may inform analysis, but SPDTF
    has not adopted or imported an upper ontology.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Identity before attributes</h2>
  <p>
    First ask whether two records refer to the same thing. Labels and field paths are weak
    evidence of identity: two meanings can share a name, and one thing can have many names.
    Record persistence, ownership, lifecycle, provenance and relationships before deciding
    whether something is a class, value or literal field.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Give independently referable things stable identifiers.</li>
    <li>Separate a thing from a document or claim that describes it.</li>
    <li>Model roles as context-dependent participation when they can change over time.</li>
    <li>Qualify a relationship when it needs dates, evidence, status or its own provenance.</li>
    <li>Prefer explicit uncertainty to a fabricated identity assertion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="construct">Class, value or relationship?</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="A reviewed meaning can call for a reusable kind, an identified instance, a governed choice, a simple value or a relationship. A relationship becomes its own resource only when the link carries business information such as dates, roles or evidence.">
flowchart TB
  accTitle: Choosing a class concept literal or qualified relationship
  accDescr: A reviewed meaning is classified as a reusable kind, identified resource, governed concept, typed literal or relationship; relationships are direct unless the link itself carries evidence or lifecycle information.
