  <table>
    <caption>Construct selection guardrails</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Construct</th><th scope="col">Use when</th><th scope="col">Avoid when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">OWL class</th><td>Instances share a reviewed kind or contextual role with defined membership.</td><td>The item is only a code, status or convenient grouping.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SKOS concept</th><td>A governed value or subject concept needs labels, codes and scheme membership.</td><td>Instances have their own lifecycle and relationships.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Datatype property</th><td>A resource has a simple typed value.</td><td>The value needs its own provenance, reuse or relationships.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Object property</th><td>A direct named relationship is sufficient.</td><td>The participation needs role, dates, evidence or status.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Qualified relationship</th><td>The link is a business record in its own right.</td><td>A simple direct edge completely expresses the meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Class membership does not imply that every class is an essential kind. A role such
    as buyer can be temporary and transaction-dependent (in ontology terminology,
    <em>anti-rigid</em>): a person can stop being a buyer without ceasing to be a person.
    The model records the role and its context without confusing it with identity.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reuse">Reuse, reference, map or mint</h2>
  <p>
    Every external-standard relationship uses one explicit mechanism. <strong>Reuse</strong>
    uses exact external terms in the package. <strong>Reference</strong> uses a standard as
    design evidence without importing its vocabulary. <strong>Map</strong> keeps local
    terms and records a qualified relationship. <strong>Mint</strong> creates an SPDTF term
    only after ownership, namespace, evidence and non-reuse rationale are recorded.
  </p>
  <p>
    Import nothing wholesale by default. Pin an exact version and term-level profile.
    Avoid <code>owl:sameAs</code> unless identity is genuinely established; use SKOS mapping
    relations or mapping metadata only at the strength supported by review evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="upper">Upper-ontology lenses</h2>
  <p>
    An upper or foundational ontology supplies general distinctions—such as object,
    event, role, quality or participation—that can help different domains model
    consistently. A modelling method can use those questions without importing an upper
    ontology into every runtime graph.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Current SPDTF upper-ontology and modelling-method disposition</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Framework</th><th scope="col">Possible role</th><th scope="col">Current standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">UFO</th><td>Design-time analysis of identity, roles, relators and category distinctions.</td><td>Method candidate; not adopted.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">OntoClean</th><td>Quality checks for rigidity, identity and valid subsumption.</td><td>Method candidate; not adopted.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">gUFO</th><td>An OWL implementation of selected UFO distinctions.</td><td>Version 1.0.0 available; no SPDTF term-level profile and no import.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">DOLCE</th><td>Alternative foundational distinctions and historical comparison.</td><td>PDTF schema evidence only; no SPDTF disposition.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">BFO and other alternatives</th><td>Comparison where a concrete competency question requires it.</td><td>Not assessed or selected for SPDTF.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The schema-derived ontology used UFO, DOLCE and OntoClean as design lenses and some
    gUFO advisory annotations without foundational imports. That is useful prior evidence,
    not inherited SPDTF policy. A new decision must compare alternatives, pin the
    chosen method or vocabulary, show term-level use and record impact on participants.
  </p>

  <h2 id="constraints">Meaning, inference and validation</h2>
  <p>
    OWL and RDFS describe meaning in an open world: missing information is not assumed
    false. RDFS domain and range can produce type inferences; they must not be copied into
    SHACL as if they were automatically mandatory fields. SHACL supplies a declared,
    use-specific validation lens. The two artefacts stay aligned but answer different
    questions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="anti-patterns">Anti-patterns</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Creating classes for every JSON object or property path.</li>
    <li>Turning every code list into a subclass hierarchy.</li>
    <li>Using domain and range as a substitute for delivery constraints.</li>
    <li>Importing a large ontology because one term appears relevant.</li>
    <li>Using layout, colour or the same label as evidence of semantic equivalence.</li>
    <li>Hiding an unresolved distinction behind a generic “mapping”.</li>
    <li>Presenting a machine-generated construct as a working-group decision.</li>
  </ul>
